    "message": {
     "content": "### Is This a Good Offer?\n\n**As an initial offer, it is average\u2014and not compelling enough to leave Meta.** \n\nYou were headhunted, you are performing well at Meta (IC6), and you are happy where you are. Leaving a stable role with proven social capital to start fresh at Google for what is essentially a **~5% raise in Year 1** ($526k vs. ~$552k) carries unnecessary career risk with virtually no financial upside.\n\n* **Base Salary is a Step Down:** Google is offering $235,000, which is lower than your current $240,000 base and sits in the lower-middle half of their posted band ($183k\u2013$271k).\n* **Missing Sign-on Bonus:** Google frequently leaves out sign-on bonuses in initial offers to test candidate sensitivity. For senior poaching from Meta, a $0 sign-on is uncompetitive.\n* **Equity Has Room to Grow:** $1.08M over 4 years ($270k/yr annualized) is standard mid-band for L6. Strong competitive L6 PM offers in the Bay Area routinely reach **$1.25M to $1.45M** in equity.\n* **The \"Stacking Refresher\" Pitch:** While Google\u2019s equity refresher model does compound over time, refreshers are tied to performance ratings and company budgets. You should evaluate the offer based on what is **guaranteed in writing today**, not hypothetical refresher projections.\n\n---\n\n### Strategy: Negotiate Firmly\n\nBecause you did not seek this role and are content at Meta, you hold **maximum leverage**. You can afford to ask for top-of-band numbers. If they meet your targets, you get a substantial compensation upgrade; if they refuse, you stay in a role you enjoy.\n\n---\n\n### What to Ask For (and Exact Target Numbers)\n\n| Component | Google Initial Offer | Your Counter-Ask | Realistic Settle Target |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Base Salary** | $235,000 | **$260,000** | $245,000 \u2013 $255,000 |\n| **Target Bonus** | 20% ($47,000) | 20% (Standard/Fixed) | 20% (Standard/Fixed) |\n| **4-Yr RSUs** | $1,080,000 (~$270k/yr) | **$1,380,000** (~$345k/yr) | $1,250,000 \u2013 $1,320,000 |\n| **Sign-on Bonus** | $0 | **$100,000** | $50,000 \u2013 $75,000 |\n| **Estimated Year 1 TC** | **~$552,000** | **~$752,000** | **~$630,000 \u2013 $660,000** |\n\n*(Note on equity: Google often uses front-loaded vesting, such as 33/33/22/12 or 38/32/20/10. Confirm the exact vesting schedule with your recruiter, as front-loading will make Year 1 and Year 2 cash flow even higher.)*\n\n---\n\n### How to Structure Your Pitch to the Recruiter\n\n1. **Acknowledge and Validate Fit:** Reiterate enthusiasm for the team, product scope, and the L6 level.\n2. **Anchor to Opportunity Cost:** Point out that moving from a stable, well-compensated IC6 position at Meta with guaranteed vesting over the next 12 months requires a clear premium.\n3. **Focus on Equity and Sign-On First:** Google's Compensation Committee has the most flexibility on equity and sign-on. Base salary increases have stricter band limits.\n\n#### Example Script:\n\n> *\"Thank you for putting this package together. I really enjoyed meeting the team and am genuinely excited about the scope of this L6 role.*\n>\n> *That said, because I was not actively looking and am in a strong position at Meta (IC6) with roughly $530k in total annual compensation and significant upcoming vesting, this initial offer is essentially lateral for me\u2014especially with a slight drop in base salary and no sign-on bonus.*\n>\n> *For me to comfortably make the transition, I'd need the compensation to reflect the level and market top for this scope. Specifically, I'm looking for:*\n> * *A base salary of **$260,000** (to keep it competitive with the upper end of your posted band).*\n> * *An equity grant closer to **$1.38M over 4 years**.*\n> * *A sign-on bonus of **$100,000** to offset unvested equity and make the Year 1 transition compelling.*\n>\n> *If we can get the total package to that range, I would be excited to sign and lock in a start date.\"*\n\n---\n\n### Decision Rule\n\n* **Accept if:** They increase Year 1 Total Comp into the **$620k+ range** (via an equity bump to $\\ge$\\$1.25M plus a sign-on bonus of $\\ge$\\$50k) and match/exceed your current base salary ($240k+).\n* **Walk away if:** They hold firm or only offer marginal adjustments (e.g., adding $10k in base or a tiny $20k sign-on). A minimal change does not justify leaving a high-performing Meta IC6 setup.",
