     "content": "### The Bottom Line\n\n**Yes, you should strongly consider taking this offer.** \n\nBy adding a **$200,000 sign-on bonus** and matching your base salary at **$240,000**, Google responded effectively. From a pure Year 1 cash-flow perspective, this is a **~$230,000+ windfall** over your current Meta compensation.\n\n---\n\n### 1. The Financial Breakdown\n\n#### Why the $200,000 Sign-on is a \"Double Win\"\nWhen you told them you were walking away from $200,000 in unvested Meta RSUs, Google effectively **reimbursed you in liquid cash** while *still* giving you a full, unreduced 4-year equity grant ($1.08M). \n\nYou aren't just replacing your Meta equity; you are getting that $200,000 in upfront cash **plus** your new Google equity vesting concurrently.\n\n#### Year 1 Comparison\n\n| Component | Current Meta (IC6) | Revised Google Offer (L6) | Difference |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Base Salary** | $240,000 | $240,000 | $0 |\n| **Target Bonus** | ~$36,000 (15%) | $48,000 (20%) | +$12,000 |\n| **Year 1 Equity Vest** | ~$200,000 \u2013 $250,000 | $270,000 *(even)* / ~$356,000 *(front-loaded)* | +$20,000 to +$106,000 |\n| **Sign-on Bonus** | $0 | **$200,000** | +$200,000 |\n| **Year 1 Total** | **~$526,000** | **~$758,000 \u2013 ~$844,000** | **+$232,000 to +$318,000** |\n\n*(Note: Google standard vesting is typically front-loaded at **33% / 33% / 22% / 12%** or **38% / 32% / 20% / 10%**. If front-loaded, your Year 1 equity alone will be ~$356k\u2013$410k, pushing your Year 1 total compensation over **$840,000**.)*\n\n---\n\n### 2. What Happens in Years 2, 3, and 4?\n\nWhile Year 1 is an undeniable home run, you must understand the multi-year trajectory before leaving Meta:\n\n* **Year 2:** Without the sign-on bonus, your compensation will normalize to **~$558,000 \u2013 $644,000** (depending on the vesting schedule). This is still slightly higher than your current Meta pace.\n* **Years 3 & 4 (The Google \"Cliff\"):** If your grant is front-loaded, your initial grant vesting drops significantly in Years 3 and 4. Google relies heavily on **annual refreshers** to bridge this gap. \n* **The Refresher Dependency:** If you meet expectations and receive ~$200k\u2013$250k in annual refreshers (which vest over future years), your total compensation will hover steadily between **$550k and $620k** in Years 2 through 4.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Career & Culture Reality Check\n\nSince compensation is now resolved at top-tier L6 levels, the decision comes down to scope and career goals:\n\n| Dimension | Meta (IC6) | Google (L6) |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Pace & Culture** | Fast-paced, ship-first, heavy individual accountability, intense review cycles (PSC). | Consensus-driven, matrixed stakeholder management, typically steadier work-life balance. |\n| **PM Autonomy** | High ownership with engineering; product vision is heavily driven by bottom-up execution. | Requires broader cross-functional alignment, policy reviews, and executive buy-in. |\n| **Resume Value** | Having both **Meta IC6** and **Google L6** on your resume cements you as a premier Tier-1 Senior PM in Big Tech. |\n\n---\n\n### 4. Next Steps Before You Sign\n\n1. **Verify the Clawback Period:** Confirm whether the $200k sign-on has a 1-year or 2-year prorated repayment clause if you leave voluntarily. (A 1-year clawback is standard; a 2-year clawback should be prorated monthly after Year 1).\n2. **Confirm the Exact Vesting Schedule:** Ask the recruiter explicitly for the monthly/quarterly vesting percentages (e.g., 33/33/22/12 vs. 25/25/25/25).\n3. **Time Your Exit from Meta:** If you have an imminent quarterly RSU vest or annual bonus payout at Meta within the next 4\u20136 weeks, push your Google start date out so you can collect that payout before giving notice.",
